<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">April 1814 · Palace of Fontainebleau</p><h1 class="sp-title">Napoleon's Farewell to the Old Guard</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>The armies of the Sixth Coalition had taken Paris and the French Senate had dethroned Napoleon. He abdicated at the Palace of Fontainebleau, and the treaty signed on 11 April 1814 sent him into exile on Elba, with 400 men as his guard. Before he left, he took leave of the soldiers of his Old Guard.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Soldiers, I bid you farewell. For twenty years that we have been together your conduct has left me nothing to desire.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">As printed in English in The World's Famous Orations (1906), which names no translator.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“Soldiers, I bid you farewell. For twenty years that we have been together your conduct has left me nothing to desire.” — Napoleon, Napoleon's Farewell to the Old Guard" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-napoleon-farewell-to-the-guard.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec sp-ft" id="full-text"><h2>The full speech</h2><div class="sp-ft-body"><p>Soldiers, I bid you farewell. For twenty years that we have been together your conduct has left me nothing to desire. I have always found you on the road to glory. All the powers of Europe have combined in arms against me.</p><p>A few of my generals have proved untrue to their duty and to France. France herself has desired other destinies; with you and the brave men who still are faithful, I might have carried on a civil war; but France would be unhappy. Be faithful, then, to your new king, be obedient to your new commanders, and desert not our beloved country.</p><p>Do not lament my lot; I will be happy when I know that you are so. I might have died; if I consent to live, it is still to promote your glory. I will write the great things that we have achieved.</p><p>I can not embrace you all, but I embrace your general. Come, General Petit, that I may press you to my heart! Bring me the eagle, that I may embrace it also! Ah! dear eagle, may this kiss which I give thee find an echo to the latest posterity! Adieu, my children; the best wishes of my heart shall be always with you: do not forget me!</p></div><p class="sp-ft-credit">Text: <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/To_His_Soldiers_at_Fontainebleau" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>, from The World's Famous Orations (1906). The text is in the public domain; the Wikisource transcription is shared under CC BY-SA 4.0.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Napoleon went into exile on Elba, which the treaty made a principality for him to rule. He escaped in 1815 and the Guard was raised again; at Waterloo the Old Guard held its formation and covered the retreat of the army before it was almost annihilated.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Battle of Waterloo</a></div></div></section>
